{"name":"Champion Blue","hex":"#606788","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"champion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ongoing winner in a game or contest.","Someone who is chosen to represent a group of people in a contest."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Acting as a champion; having defeated all one's competitors.","Excellent; beyond compare."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/champion"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Champion","blocks":[{"text":"A champion is the victor in a challenge, contest or competition. There can be a territorial pyramid of championships, e.g. local, regional/provincial/state, national, continental and world championships, and even further (artificial) divisions at one or more of these levels, as in association football. Their champions can be accordingly styled, e.g. national champion, world champion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/River_Plate_campe%C3%B3n_de_Am%C3%A9rica_1986.png/330px-River_Plate_campe%C3%B3n_de_Am%C3%A9rica_1986.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Champion","wikidata":"Q852061"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Beowulf_-_cempan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Beowulf_-_cempan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"\"cempa\" or \"cempan\" stood for 'warrior' in the ancient Anglo-Saxon texts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_cempan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Champions_2009.JPG/500px-Champions_2009.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Champions_2009.JPG/1280px-Champions_2009.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Champions_2009.JPG/500px-Champions_2009.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Final Day of the 2009 season, Fitzroy celebrate their first championship in nine years.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChampions_2009.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Spinner-Champions.jpg/500px-Spinner-Champions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik